WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.670 --> 00:00:04.059
SPEAKER: So I promised
a demo of App Engine

00:00:04.059 --> 00:00:05.410
in action taking load.

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:10.450
So-- oops-- there's
my Kubernetes app.

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:11.440
It decided to load.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:12.820
OK.

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:16.570
So I have here a set of 10 VMs--

00:00:16.570 --> 00:00:17.564
Compute Engine VMs.

00:00:17.564 --> 00:00:18.980
And they're going
to hit that URL.

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:20.230
Please-- you can hit the URL.

00:00:20.230 --> 00:00:20.780
Don't hit that URL.

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:21.430
No, no.

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:25.240
Don't, because you'll break
the whole-- like, from cold,

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:28.450
we're going to see
how App Engine scales.

00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:31.250
So you can hit it after
I'm done with the demo,

00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:33.710
if that's your thing.

00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:37.970
So I'm going to go ahead
and run one make command.

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:39.500
And I'll explain what I'm doing.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:41.210
I'm instructing
each one of my VMs

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:48.500
to send 1,000 hits at that
URL and to do it concurrently

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:49.640
at 50 apiece--

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:52.830
so a 500 QPS query.

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:53.990
So I'm going to run that.

00:00:53.990 --> 00:00:55.448
I'm going to switch
back over here.

00:00:55.448 --> 00:00:57.200
Someone hit the URL!

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:58.680
Someone did it!

00:00:58.680 --> 00:00:59.934
That's all right.

00:00:59.934 --> 00:01:01.100
You'll still see some magic.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:02.690
So in a moment,
they're going to see,

00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:05.540
as soon as Compute
Engine gets the traffic

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:07.520
and starts sending it,
they start bouncing.

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:09.020
And then it's App
Engine spinning up

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:11.420
instances of my
application in order

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:13.400
to handle the load
that's coming in.

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:16.040
And basically what will happen
is each instance can handle

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:18.239
a certain amount of traffic
at once, and then say,

00:01:18.239 --> 00:01:19.280
hey, I'm a little pegged.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:21.529
And we'll start spitting up
more instances to take it.

00:01:21.529 --> 00:01:24.200
That's how quickly we
spit up instances in order

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:25.672
to deal with your traffic.

00:01:25.672 --> 00:01:27.130
And that's why the
whole scale to 0

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:31.520
is awesome, because it quickly
responds when you have traffic.

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:33.350
But then, when we
don't have traffic,

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:35.960
it starts spinning
down instances.

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:37.960
And three of you--

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:39.700
your queues-- three of you--

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:40.280
four!

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:40.780
All right.

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:41.590
Well, now I don't care.

00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:43.423
Now, you can just ping
it away all you want.

00:01:43.423 --> 00:01:45.780
And you can see the
numbers go up in real time.

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:46.280
Yeah.

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:47.390
Yeah, you're really doing that.

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:48.640
You're really hitting the URL.

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:50.600
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:52.010
Oh, all right!

00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:53.690
I've challenged people there.

00:01:53.690 --> 00:01:54.930
They're feeling feisty.

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:59.110
So that's just a quick demo
of what App Engine can do.

